#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98c65517-e27d-5eb7-b458-8dcf5425928f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100273" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f5e618-9958-5c65-98d7-f72834d8cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052636} {:answer/id #uuid "c02dfdc4-656f-5641-998f-9bc8a0a18e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052637} {:answer/id #uuid "d027fbd4-28ba-5990-9755-63cf439bf5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052638} {:answer/id #uuid "41356929-64d9-5d70-95a1-343710b86dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052639} {:answer/id #uuid "42e68d28-554b-55d7-b11f-12bfa298f433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052640}] :answer-field/id #uuid "022ec75f-d363-5486-95b7-fc4dff138c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052635}] :question/id #uuid "0bbcd500-ae70-509c-b570-d85bc1475ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35294" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind $lim_(x → - 1)(5 + 7x)^{3}(f(x) - x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Different Rules to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946e7009-82f2-5bbe-a154-157adaaa3621" :question/math-academy-id "q-35295" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}f(x)=1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}(4(f(x))^{4}+10x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(1)^{4}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
